const a=[{slug:"madagascar-2-week-itinerary",title:"Madagascar Itinerary: How to Spend 2 Weeks (Baobabs, Tsingy, Lemurs & Beach)",excerpt:"Two weeks is the sweet spot for Madagascar. Here's a realistic day-by-day plan combining the Avenue of the Baobabs, Tsingy de Bemaraha, Andasibe's lemurs and a few days on an island.",cover:"/images/photos/complete-western-circuit-kiritour-madagascar-f085a.webp",date:"2026-10-15",readTime:"9 min",category:"Itineraries",featured:!0,keywords:"madagascar itinerary 2 weeks, 14 days madagascar, madagascar travel plan, madagascar route, baobabs tsingy andasibe itinerary",relatedTour:"western-madagascar-5-day-circuit-baobabs-kirindy",relatedTourLabel:"Western Madagascar 5-Day Circuit",facts:[{label:"Ideal length",value:"14 days"},{label:"Best season",value:"April – November"},{label:"Regions",value:"West, rainforest, island"},{label:"Getting around",value:"Private 4x4 + 2–3 domestic flights"}],content:[{type:"p",text:"Madagascar is bigger than France, and its roads are slow. The most common mistake first-time visitors make is trying to see everything in ten days and spending most of it in a car. Two weeks lets you see the island's three great experiences — the baobabs and Tsingy of the west, the rainforest lemurs of the east, and a few days of rest by the sea — at a pace you'll actually enjoy."},{type:"h2",text:"The Short Version"},{type:"ul",items:["Days 1–2: Antananarivo and Andasibe rainforest (indri lemurs)","Days 3–4: Fly to Morondava — Avenue of the Baobabs at sunset","Days 5–8: Tsingy de Bemaraha and Kirindy Forest (4x4 loop)","Days 9–10: Morondava and the coast, or the Tsiribihina River","Days 11–14: Beach time on Nosy Be or Sainte-Marie, then fly home"]},{type:"h2",text:"Days 1–2: Antananarivo and Andasibe"},{type:"p",text:"Most international flights land in Antananarivo. Rather than staying in the capital, many travellers drive east the next morning to Andasibe, about four hours away. This is where you hear the call of the indri, the largest living lemur, echoing through the rainforest at dawn. A night walk reveals chameleons, frogs and tiny nocturnal lemurs."},{type:"tour",slug:"andasibe-rainforest-indri-lemurs-3-day-tour",label:"Andasibe 3-Day Rainforest Tour",note:"Indri lemurs, Mantadia and a night walk"},{type:"h2",text:"Days 3–4: Morondava and the Avenue of the Baobabs"},{type:"p",text:"Return to Antananarivo and take the short domestic flight to Morondava on the west coast — a completely different Madagascar of dry forests, red tracks and giant baobabs. Arrive in time for sunset at the Avenue of the Baobabs, then spend the next day exploring Betania fishing village and Kimony beach."},{type:"tip",text:"The overland route to Morondava takes two long days. If your time is limited, the flight saves you a full day of driving each way."},{type:"tour",slug:"avenue-baobabs-betania-kimony-day-tour-morondava",label:"Baobabs, Betania & Kimony Day Tour",note:"Sunset at the Avenue and a day on the coast"},{type:"h2",text:"Days 5–8: Tsingy de Bemaraha and Kirindy Forest"},{type:"p",text:"This is the adventure part of the trip. A private 4x4 takes you north from Morondava, across two river ferries, to Bekopaka — the gateway to the Tsingy de Bemaraha UNESCO site. You'll hike the Grand and Petit Tsingy with harnesses and suspension bridges, then drive back south to Kirindy Forest for a night walk and the chance to spot the fossa."},{type:"tour",slug:"tsingy-de-bemaraha-4-day-unesco-kirindy-baobabs",label:"Tsingy de Bemaraha 4-Day Circuit",note:"Grand & Petit Tsingy, Kirindy and the baobabs"},{type:"h2",text:"Days 9–10: Slow Down on the West Coast"},{type:"p",text:"After four days on the road, keep two days flexible. Relax in Morondava, explore the mangroves by pirogue, or — if you have more energy — swap this part for a Tsiribihina River descent, floating past Sakalava villages and camping on sandbanks."},{type:"h2",text:"Days 11–14: Finish on an Island"},{type:"p",text:"End your trip with a few days by the sea. Nosy Be in the north-west has calm water and easy island-hopping; Sainte-Marie on the east coast is quieter and famous for humpback whales, mainly from July to September. Both are reached by domestic flight via Antananarivo."},{type:"ask",text:"Want this two-week plan with your own dates, flights and hotels? Tell us when you travel and we'll send a full itinerary and price."},{type:"h2",text:"What If You Have 10 Days or 3 Weeks?"},{type:"ul",items:["10 days: choose one region — the west (baobabs, Tsingy, Kirindy) or Andasibe + the RN7.","2 weeks: the plan above, flying between regions.","3 weeks: add the RN7 road trip through Antsirabe, Ranomafana and Isalo."]}],faq:[{q:"How many days do you need in Madagascar?",a:"Ten days is the minimum for one region. Two weeks lets you combine the west (baobabs, Tsingy) with the rainforest and a beach. Three weeks or more lets you add the RN7 road trip in the south."},{q:"What is the best order for a Madagascar itinerary?",a:"Start with Andasibe close to Antananarivo, fly to Morondava for the baobabs and Tsingy, and finish with a few relaxing days on Nosy Be or Sainte-Marie before your flight home."},{q:"Can you visit Tsingy de Bemaraha all year?",a:"No. Tsingy is usually only accessible in the dry season, roughly April to November. The roads become impassable during the rains."}]},{slug:"best-time-to-visit-madagascar",title:"Best Time to Visit Madagascar: Month-by-Month Guide by Region",excerpt:"Madagascar's weather changes a lot from region to region. Here's when to go for the baobabs and Tsingy, the rainforest lemurs, the RN7 and the islands — and which months to avoid.",cover:"/images/photos/golden-hour-baobabs-kiritour-madagascar-cbfd5.webp",date:"2026-10-14",readTime:"7 min",category:"Travel Guide",keywords:"best time to visit madagascar, madagascar weather, madagascar dry season, when to go madagascar, madagascar rainy season, madagascar by month",relatedTour:"tsingy-de-bemaraha-4-day-unesco-kirindy-baobabs",relatedTourLabel:"Tsingy de Bemaraha 4-Day Circuit",facts:[{label:"Best overall",value:"April – November"},{label:"Our favourite month",value:"October"},{label:"Busiest",value:"July – August"},{label:"Avoid for Tsingy",value:"December – March"}],content:[{type:"p",text:"The short answer: the best time to visit Madagascar is the dry season, from April to November. But the island is huge, and each region has its own rhythm. If you know what you want to see, you can pick the perfect month."},{type:"h2",text:"Dry Season vs Rainy Season"},{type:"ul",items:["April to November — dry season: cooler, sunny, roads open. The best time for most trips.","December to March — rainy season: hot and humid, with heavy rain and cyclone risk. Many western roads and the Tsingy are closed."]},{type:"h2",text:"The West: Baobabs, Tsingy and Kirindy"},{type:"p",text:"Western Madagascar is the most weather-dependent region. The Avenue of the Baobabs can be visited most of the year, but the dirt roads to Tsingy de Bemaraha and Kirindy are only reliably open in the dry season. Tsingy is usually closed during the rainy months."},{type:"ul",items:["Best: May to October — clear skies and spectacular baobab sunsets","Good: April and November — fewer visitors, but check road conditions","Avoid for Tsingy: December to March"]},{type:"tour",slug:"avenue-baobabs-betania-kimony-day-tour-morondava",label:"Avenue of the Baobabs Day Tour",note:"Possible most of the year from Morondava"},{type:"h2",text:"The Rainforest: Andasibe"},{type:"p",text:"Rainforests are, by definition, wet all year. Andasibe can be visited in any month, but the drier months from September to November are pleasant and lemurs are active. Bring a rain jacket whenever you go."},{type:"h2",text:"The South: RN7, Ranomafana and Isalo"},{type:"p",text:"The classic RN7 road trip is best from April to November. The highlands around Antsirabe can be surprisingly cold at night in June and July, while Isalo and the south-west coast stay warm and dry."},{type:"h2",text:"The Islands: Nosy Be and Sainte-Marie"},{type:"p",text:"Nosy Be is at its best from about May to October, with calm seas for island-hopping. Sainte-Marie is famous for humpback whales, which gather in its channel from roughly June to September. Both islands are wetter from December to March."},{type:"tip",text:"July and August are the busiest months. If you travel then, book your tour and domestic flights early — vehicles and good lodges fill up."},{type:"h2",text:"Our Favourite Months"},{type:"p",text:"If we had to choose, we'd say October: the weather is dry, the baobabs glow at sunset, lemurs are active, and it's quieter than the July–August peak. May and June are also excellent, with green landscapes just after the rains."}],faq:[{q:"What is the best month to visit Madagascar?",a:"October is often considered ideal: dry, warm and less crowded than July–August. May and June are also very good."},{q:"Is it worth visiting Madagascar in the rainy season?",a:"It can be, for the rainforest or a city stay, but many roads in the west — including the route to Tsingy — close between December and March, and there is a cyclone risk."},{q:"When can you see whales in Madagascar?",a:"Humpback whales visit Sainte-Marie from roughly June to September, and the waters around Nosy Be later in the season."}]},{slug:"where-to-see-lemurs-in-madagascar",title:"Where to See Lemurs in Madagascar: The 7 Best Places",excerpt:"From the singing indri of Andasibe to the dancing sifakas of the west, here are the best places in Madagascar to see lemurs in the wild — and how to combine them in one trip.",cover:"/images/photos/sifaka-kiritour-madagascar-2a667.webp",date:"2026-10-13",readTime:"8 min",category:"Wildlife",keywords:"where to see lemurs, lemurs madagascar, best place lemurs, indri andasibe, sifaka, lemur tour madagascar, ring-tailed lemur",relatedTour:"andasibe-rainforest-indri-lemurs-3-day-tour",relatedTourLabel:"Andasibe 3-Day Rainforest Tour",facts:[{label:"Easiest",value:"Andasibe (4h from Tana)"},{label:"Near Morondava",value:"Kirindy Forest"},{label:"Best time of day",value:"Early morning & dusk"},{label:"Season",value:"All year (dry season easier)"}],content:[{type:"p",text:"Lemurs live only in Madagascar, and seeing them in the wild is the reason many travellers come. There are many species, and each region has its own. Here are the places where your chances are best."},{type:"h2",text:"1. Andasibe — The Indri"},{type:"p",text:"Andasibe, about four hours east of Antananarivo, is the most famous lemur destination in the country. It is home to the indri, the largest living lemur, whose haunting song carries for kilometres through the forest. You can also see the diademed sifaka in nearby Mantadia, plus many smaller species on night walks."},{type:"tour",slug:"andasibe-rainforest-indri-lemurs-3-day-tour",label:"Andasibe 3-Day Rainforest Tour",note:"Analamazaotra, Mantadia and a night walk"},{type:"h2",text:"2. Kirindy Forest — Sifakas and the Mouse Lemur"},{type:"p",text:"In the dry forest of Kirindy, near Morondava, you'll find Verreaux's sifaka and red-fronted brown lemurs by day. At night, guides help you find the mouse lemur, one of the smallest primates in the world. Kirindy is also the best place in Madagascar to look for the fossa, the lemurs' main predator."},{type:"tour",slug:"kirindy-forest-1-day-tour-from-morondava",label:"Kirindy Forest Day Tour",note:"From Morondava, with a baobabs sunset"},{type:"h2",text:"3. Tsingy de Bemaraha"},{type:"p",text:"Between the limestone pinnacles of the Tsingy live Decken's sifakas, with their cream-white fur, and red-fronted brown lemurs. Seeing a sifaka leap across the stone forest is unforgettable."},{type:"h2",text:"4. Palmarium — The Aye-Aye"},{type:"p",text:"On the Pangalanes Canal on the east coast, the Palmarium reserve is one of the few places where travellers can see the strange, nocturnal aye-aye on an evening excursion."},{type:"tour",slug:"andasibe-palmarium-aye-aye-5-day-madagascar",label:"Andasibe + Palmarium 5 Days",note:"Rainforest lemurs and the aye-aye"},{type:"h2",text:"5. Ranomafana"},{type:"p",text:"Along the RN7, Ranomafana's misty rainforest is known for its bamboo lemurs, including the rare golden bamboo lemur."},{type:"h2",text:"6. Isalo"},{type:"p",text:"In the canyons of Isalo National Park in the south, you can often see ring-tailed lemurs and Verreaux's sifakas around the campsites and natural pools."},{type:"h2",text:"7. Nosy Be"},{type:"p",text:"On Nosy Be, the Lokobe reserve and the nearby island of Nosy Komba are known for black lemurs."},{type:"tip",text:"Lemurs are most active early in the morning and late in the afternoon. A local guide knows their territories and greatly increases your chances of good sightings."},{type:"h2",text:"How to Combine Lemur Hotspots"},{type:"p",text:"A popular plan is to start with Andasibe for the indri, then fly to Morondava for Kirindy and the Tsingy. In one to two weeks you can see several very different species in completely different landscapes."}],faq:[{q:"Where is the best place to see lemurs in Madagascar?",a:"Andasibe is the easiest and most famous, home to the indri. Kirindy Forest in the west is excellent for sifakas, nocturnal species and the fossa."},{q:"Can you see lemurs near Morondava?",a:"Yes. Kirindy Forest, about two hours from Morondava, has sifakas, brown lemurs and mouse lemurs. It's an easy day trip or overnight tour."},{q:"When is the best time to see lemurs?",a:"Lemurs can be seen all year. The dry season (April to November) makes travel easier, and early mornings and late afternoons are the best times of day."}]},{slug:"madagascar-tour-cost",title:"How Much Does a Madagascar Tour Cost? Real Prices for 2026",excerpt:"What should you budget for a private tour in Madagascar? We break down real prices for day trips, Tsingy circuits and river descents, and explain what makes a tour cheaper or more expensive.",cover:"/images/photos/limestone-cathedrals-and-wildlife-kiritour-madagascar-eccbe.webp",date:"2026-10-12",readTime:"7 min",category:"Travel Tips",keywords:"madagascar tour cost, madagascar trip price, madagascar budget, how much madagascar tour, tsingy tour price, morondava tour price",relatedTour:"tsingy-de-bemaraha-3-day-express-circuit-madagascar",relatedTourLabel:"Tsingy de Bemaraha 3-Day Express",facts:[{label:"Day tours from",value:"145€"},{label:"Tsingy 3 days from",value:"369€"},{label:"Biggest cost",value:"Private 4x4 & driver"},{label:"Best way to save",value:"Travel as 3–4 people"}],content:[{type:"p",text:"Prices for Madagascar tours vary a lot, and it's often hard to compare offers. Here we share our own published prices, so you can see what a private tour really costs and what is included."},{type:"h2",text:"Our Prices at a Glance"},{type:"ul",items:["Avenue of the Baobabs, Betania & Kimony day tour: 145€ – 205€","Kirindy Forest day tour from Morondava: 191€ – 224€","Tsingy de Bemaraha 3 days: from 369€ (car hire) to 965€ (with excursions and guide)","Tsingy de Bemaraha 4 days with Kirindy and baobabs: from 488€","Tsiribihina River 3-day descent: 529€ (1 person)","Andasibe rainforest 3 days from Antananarivo: 996€ (1 person)"]},{type:"p",text:"Prices depend on your group size and the package you choose. Every tour page shows the full price table and what is and isn't included."},{type:"tour",slug:"avenue-baobabs-betania-kimony-day-tour-morondava",label:"Baobabs, Betania & Kimony Day Tour",note:"Our most affordable tour"},{type:"h2",text:"Why Group Size Changes the Price"},{type:"p",text:"On a private tour, the biggest costs — the 4x4, the driver, fuel and the guide — are shared by everyone in the group. That's why travelling as a small group is much better value than travelling alone. Each tour page shows the price for one traveller and for a group, and we confirm the exact price for your group size and needs."},{type:"h2",text:"What Is Usually Included"},{type:"ul",items:["Private 4x4 vehicle with driver and fuel","A local guide (depending on the package)","Park entrance and local guide fees on full packages","Camping equipment and crew on river descents"]},{type:"h2",text:"What Is Usually Not Included"},{type:"ul",items:["International and domestic flights","Travel insurance","Some meals and drinks (depending on the package)","Tips for guides and drivers"]},{type:"tip",text:"When comparing quotes, always check whether park fees, the local guide and accommodation are included. A cheaper price often leaves these out."},{type:"h2",text:"How to Save Money"},{type:"ul",items:["Travel as a group of 3–4 to share the vehicle cost","Choose the 'car + guide' package if you don't need full board","Travel in May, June, September or October rather than the July–August peak","Combine tours: a multi-day circuit is cheaper than several separate trips"]},{type:"ask",text:"Send us your dates and group size — we'll give you an exact price, with everything included clearly listed."}],faq:[{q:"How much is a day tour to the Avenue of the Baobabs?",a:"Our Baobabs, Betania and Kimony day tour costs 145€ to 205€, depending on group size."},{q:"How much does a Tsingy de Bemaraha tour cost?",a:"Our 3-day Tsingy circuits start at 369€ for car hire only, and from 597€ with excursions and guide. Prices depend on group size and package."},{q:"Is Madagascar expensive to travel?",a:"Daily costs are low, but distances are long and remote areas need a 4x4, which is the main expense. Travelling in a small group makes private tours much more affordable."}]},{slug:"rn7-road-trip-madagascar",title:"RN7 Road Trip Madagascar: Antananarivo to Tuléar Guide",excerpt:"The RN7 is Madagascar's classic road trip — from the highlands of Antsirabe to the rainforest of Ranomafana, the canyons of Isalo and the beaches near Tuléar. Here's how to plan it.",cover:null,date:"2026-10-11",readTime:"8 min",category:"Itineraries",keywords:"rn7 madagascar, rn7 road trip, antananarivo to tulear, isalo national park, ranomafana, antsirabe, madagascar south tour",relatedTour:null,facts:[{label:"Distance",value:"Close to 1,000 km"},{label:"Time needed",value:"7–12 days"},{label:"Best season",value:"April – November"},{label:"Highlights",value:"Ranomafana, Isalo"}],content:[{type:"p",text:"The Route Nationale 7 runs south from Antananarivo to Tuléar on the south-west coast. Along the way it crosses rice-terraced highlands, craft towns, misty rainforest and the dramatic sandstone canyons of Isalo. It is the most popular road trip in Madagascar — and for good reason."},{type:"h2",text:"The Main Stops"},{type:"ul",items:["Antsirabe — a highland town of rickshaws, thermal springs and craft workshops","Ambositra — Madagascar's woodcarving capital","Ranomafana — rainforest national park, bamboo lemurs and hot springs","Fianarantsoa — old town and the surrounding tea and wine region","Andringitra (optional) — granite peaks and serious hiking","Isalo — sandstone canyons, natural swimming pools and ring-tailed lemurs","Tuléar and Ifaty — the end of the road, with beaches and reefs"]},{type:"h2",text:"How Many Days Do You Need?"},{type:"p",text:"The road from Antananarivo to Tuléar is close to 1,000 km, and much of it is slow. Plan at least 7 to 8 days for the main stops, or 10 to 12 days if you want to hike in Ranomafana, Andringitra and Isalo without rushing. Most travellers fly back to Antananarivo from Tuléar rather than driving the same road twice."},{type:"tip",text:"Driving at night in Madagascar is not recommended. A good RN7 itinerary keeps daily drives short enough to arrive before dark."},{type:"h2",text:"Combine the RN7 with the West"},{type:"p",text:"From Antsirabe, a road leads west through Miandrivazo to Morondava. This means you can combine the highlands with the Tsiribihina River, the baobabs and Kirindy — or go down the RN7 first and fly to Morondava afterwards."},{type:"tour",slug:"menabe-grand-tour-8-day-madagascar-adventure",label:"Epic Menabe Grand Tour — 8 Days",note:"Antsirabe, Tsiribihina, Tsingy, Kirindy and the baobabs"},{type:"h2",text:"Best Time for the RN7"},{type:"p",text:"April to November is best. June and July nights in the highlands can be cold, so pack a warm layer. The rainy season makes hiking in Ranomafana muddy and can damage some sections of road."},{type:"h2",text:"Private Driver or Self-Drive?"},{type:"p",text:"Most visitors travel the RN7 with a private car and driver. Drivers know the road, the safe stops and the best local guides in each park, which lets you enjoy the scenery instead of watching potholes."},{type:"ask",text:"We organise RN7 trips on request, with your own vehicle and driver. Tell us your dates and how many days you have — we'll send a plan and a price."}],faq:[{q:"How long does the RN7 road trip take?",a:"At least 7–8 days for the main stops between Antananarivo and Tuléar, or 10–12 days with hiking in Ranomafana, Andringitra and Isalo."},{q:"Can you combine the RN7 with Morondava?",a:"Yes. From Antsirabe you can drive west via Miandrivazo to Morondava, or fly to Morondava after your RN7 trip."},{q:"Where can you see lemurs on the RN7?",a:"Ranomafana for bamboo lemurs and Isalo for ring-tailed lemurs and sifakas are the main stops."}]},{slug:"nosy-be-or-sainte-marie",title:"Nosy Be or Sainte-Marie? Which Madagascar Island to Choose",excerpt:"Ending your Madagascar trip on an island? Here's how Nosy Be and Sainte-Marie compare — beaches, whales, atmosphere, best season — so you can choose the right one.",cover:null,date:"2026-10-10",readTime:"6 min",category:"Islands",keywords:"nosy be or sainte marie, nosy be, sainte marie madagascar, ile sainte marie whales, madagascar beach, madagascar island",relatedTour:null,facts:[{label:"Nosy Be best",value:"May – October"},{label:"Whales at Sainte-Marie",value:"≈ July – September"},{label:"Getting there",value:"Flight via Antananarivo"},{label:"Stay at least",value:"3–4 nights"}],content:[{type:"p",text:"After the dusty roads of the west or the RN7, a few days on an island is the perfect way to finish a Madagascar trip. The two favourites are Nosy Be in the north-west and Sainte-Marie on the east coast. They are very different."},{type:"h2",text:"Nosy Be in a Nutshell"},{type:"ul",items:["The most popular beach destination in Madagascar, with the widest choice of hotels","Calm, clear water and easy boat trips to Nosy Komba, Nosy Tanikely and Nosy Iranja","Black lemurs in the Lokobe reserve and on Nosy Komba","Best from about May to October; rainy from December to March"]},{type:"h2",text:"Sainte-Marie in a Nutshell"},{type:"ul",items:["A long, green and quiet island — more relaxed and less developed than Nosy Be","Humpback whales in the channel, mainly from July to September (sometimes from late June)","The tiny car-free Île aux Nattes at its southern tip","Wetter than the west all year, so expect some showers"]},{type:"h2",text:"Which One Should You Choose?"},{type:"p",text:"Choose Nosy Be if you want classic beach holidays, boat excursions and lots of choice. Choose Sainte-Marie if you travel between July and September and want to see whales, or if you prefer a quiet, authentic island atmosphere."},{type:"tip",text:"Both islands are reached by domestic flight via Antananarivo. Flights are limited, so book them together with the rest of your itinerary."},{type:"h2",text:"How Many Days?"},{type:"p",text:"Three to four nights is a good minimum to make the flight worthwhile and enjoy a couple of boat trips. Five or more nights is perfect if this is the relaxing end of a long trip."},{type:"h2",text:"Combine an Island with the Baobabs"},{type:"p",text:"A classic plan is to start with Andasibe, fly to Morondava for the baobabs, Kirindy and Tsingy, then fly to Nosy Be or Sainte-Marie for the last days. We can organise the whole route for you."},{type:"ask",text:"We organise Nosy Be and Sainte-Marie stays on request — alone or combined with the west. Tell us your dates and we'll suggest the best island for your trip."}],faq:[{q:"Is Nosy Be or Sainte-Marie better?",a:"Nosy Be is better for beaches, boat trips and choice of hotels. Sainte-Marie is better for whale watching (mainly July to September) and a quieter atmosphere."},{q:"When is whale season in Sainte-Marie?",a:"Humpback whales gather in the Sainte-Marie channel mainly from July to September, sometimes from late June."},{q:"How do you get to Nosy Be or Sainte-Marie?",a:"By domestic flight via Antananarivo. Sainte-Marie can also be reached by road and boat from the east coast."}]},{slug:"antananarivo-city-tour",title:"Antananarivo in One Day: What to See in Madagascar's Capital",excerpt:"Have a day in Antananarivo before or after your tour? Here's how to spend it: the Queen's Palace, the old town, markets, Lake Anosy and the royal hill of Ambohimanga.",cover:null,date:"2026-10-09",readTime:"6 min",category:"Travel Guide",keywords:"antananarivo city tour, what to see antananarivo, tana city tour, rova antananarivo, ambohimanga, antananarivo one day",relatedTour:null,facts:[{label:"Time needed",value:"1 full day"},{label:"Must-see",value:"The Rova (Queen's Palace)"},{label:"Half-day trip",value:"Ambohimanga (UNESCO)"},{label:"Best with",value:"Andasibe or a flight west"}],content:[{type:"p",text:"Most trips to Madagascar start and end in Antananarivo, the capital everyone calls 'Tana'. Many travellers rush through it, but the city, built on hills above rice fields, deserves at least a day."},{type:"h2",text:"Morning: The Rova and the Old Town"},{type:"p",text:"Start at the top of the city with the Rova, the royal compound often called the Queen's Palace, which overlooks the whole valley. Its museum, the wooden Besakana palace and the royal tombs take about two hours — note that the Rova is closed on Tuesdays. Walk down through the old Andohalo district with its cobbled streets, churches and viewpoints."},{type:"h2",text:"Midday: Lake Anosy and Lunch"},{type:"p",text:"Lake Anosy, with its jacaranda trees (beautiful in flower around October and November) and war memorial, is a calm spot in the city centre. It's a good moment to try Malagasy dishes like romazava or ravitoto."},{type:"h2",text:"Afternoon: Markets and Crafts"},{type:"ul",items:["Analakely — the busy central market area","The craft market on the airport road (La Digue) — embroidery, wood carvings, raffia and gemstones"]},{type:"tip",text:"Keep valuables out of sight in crowded markets and avoid walking alone after dark. A local guide makes the city both easier and safer to explore."},{type:"h2",text:"Half-Day Trips from Tana"},{type:"ul",items:["Ambohimanga — a UNESCO-listed royal hill about 20 km north of the city","Lemurs' Park — west of the city, an easy first look at lemurs"]},{type:"h2",text:"Combine Tana with Andasibe or the West"},{type:"p",text:"A city day fits naturally at the start of an Andasibe rainforest tour or before your flight to Morondava for the baobabs."},{type:"tour",slug:"andasibe-rainforest-indri-lemurs-3-day-tour",label:"Andasibe 3-Day Rainforest Tour",note:"Departs from Antananarivo"},{type:"ask",text:"We organise Antananarivo city tours with a car, driver and local guide on request. Tell us your arrival date."}],faq:[{q:"Is one day enough for Antananarivo?",a:"One full day is enough to see the Rova, the old town, Lake Anosy and a craft market. Add half a day for Ambohimanga."},{q:"What is the Rova in Antananarivo?",a:"The Rova is the former royal compound on the highest hill of the city, often called the Queen's Palace, with panoramic views."}]},{slug:"best-time-visit-avenue-baobabs-madagascar",title:"Best Time to Visit the Avenue of the Baobabs (2026 Guide)",excerpt:"Planning to see Madagascar's most iconic sunset? Here's exactly when to visit the Avenue of the Baobabs for the best light, weather and fewest crowds.",cover:"/images/photos/tsingy-de-bemaraha-kiritour-madagascar-87b10.webp",date:"2026-06-01",updated:"2026-10-15",readTime:"6 min",category:"Travel Guide",keywords:"when to visit baobabs, avenue of the baobabs best time, morondava weather, madagascar dry season, baobabs sunset",relatedTour:"avenue-baobabs-betania-kimony-day-tour-morondava",relatedTourLabel:"Avenue of the Baobabs Day Tour",facts:[{label:"Best months",value:"April – November"},{label:"Best time of day",value:"Sunset (and sunrise)"},{label:"From Morondava",value:"≈ 20 km · 30–45 min"},{label:"Time needed",value:"1–2 hours at sunset"}],content:[{type:"p",text:"The Avenue of the Baobabs near Morondava is one of Madagascar's most photographed places — a row of ancient, towering baobab trees that glow gold at sunset. But timing your visit matters enormously. The right month and time of day can transform an ordinary stop into an unforgettable experience."},{type:"h2",text:"The Best Months: April to November (Dry Season)"},{type:"p",text:"The dry season, from April to November, is the best time to visit the Avenue of the Baobabs. Roads to Morondava and beyond are passable, skies are clear, and the famous baobab silhouettes stand out crisply against orange sunsets. The peak window of July to September offers the most reliable weather, though it is also the busiest."},{type:"p",text:"From December to March, the rainy season makes the dirt roads to the baobabs — and especially to Tsingy and Kirindy — difficult or impossible. Some tracks become completely impassable. If you can, plan your western Madagascar trip in the dry months."},{type:"h2",text:"Best Time of Day: Sunset (and Sunrise)"},{type:"p",text:"The Avenue earns its fame at sunset, roughly between 4:30 PM and 6:00 PM depending on the season. The low sun turns the baobabs into dramatic black silhouettes against a burning sky. Arrive at least 45 minutes early to find your spot and watch the light change."},{type:"p",text:"Sunrise is the quieter alternative — fewer people, soft pink light and a peaceful atmosphere. Many travellers do both: sunrise one day, sunset another."},{type:"tip",text:"Golden hour fills up fast in peak season. A local guide knows the best vantage points away from the crowds and the exact light timing for that month."},{type:"h2",text:"Photography Tips"},{type:"ul",items:["Shoot from low down to make the trees look even taller against the sky","Stay after sunset: the blue hour gives soft, colourful skies","Bring a light tripod for sunrise and dusk shots","Be respectful of local people walking with zebu carts — always ask before taking portraits"]},{type:"h2",text:"How Long Do You Need?"},{type:"p",text:"The baobabs themselves take an hour or two at sunset. But the Avenue pairs perfectly with nearby Kirindy Forest (fossa and lemurs) and the Tsingy de Bemaraha UNESCO site. A 2 to 4 day western circuit lets you combine all the highlights of the Menabe region in one trip."},{type:"tour",slug:"avenue-baobabs-betania-kimony-day-tour-morondava",label:"Baobabs, Betania & Kimony Day Tour",note:"Coast, sacred sites and the baobabs at sunset"},{type:"h2",text:"Getting There"},{type:"p",text:"The Avenue is about 20 km north of Morondava on the road to Belo-sur-Tsiribihina — around 30 to 45 minutes by 4x4. A private guided tour handles transport, timing and photography stops so you arrive exactly when the light is best."}],faq:[{q:"What time is sunset at the Avenue of the Baobabs?",a:"Roughly between 4:30 PM and 6:00 PM depending on the season. Arrive about 45 minutes before sunset."},{q:"How far is the Avenue of the Baobabs from Morondava?",a:"About 20 km north of Morondava, around 30 to 45 minutes by 4x4."},{q:"Can you visit the baobabs in the rainy season?",a:"It's often possible, but roads can be muddy and the routes further north to Kirindy and Tsingy may be closed. The dry season, April to November, is best."}]},{slug:"tsingy-de-bemaraha-travel-guide",title:"Tsingy de Bemaraha: Complete Travel Guide 2026",excerpt:"Everything you need to know to visit Tsingy de Bemaraha — Madagascar's UNESCO stone forest: how to get there, what to expect, difficulty and the best tours.",cover:"/images/photos/limestone-cathedrals-and-wildlife-kiritour-madagascar-eccbe.webp",date:"2026-06-03",updated:"2026-10-15",readTime:"8 min",category:"Travel Guide",keywords:"tsingy de bemaraha, how to visit tsingy, unesco madagascar, bekopaka, grand tsingy, tsingy tour",relatedTour:"tsingy-de-bemaraha-4-day-unesco-kirindy-baobabs",relatedTourLabel:"Tsingy de Bemaraha 4-Day Tour",facts:[{label:"Status",value:"UNESCO World Heritage"},{label:"Gateway",value:"Bekopaka"},{label:"Open",value:"Dry season (≈ April – November)"},{label:"Time needed",value:"3–4 days from Morondava"}],content:[{type:"p",text:"Tsingy de Bemaraha is one of the most extraordinary landscapes on Earth — a vast forest of razor-sharp limestone pinnacles that rise like a stone cathedral. A UNESCO World Heritage Site, it sits in western Madagascar and rewards the adventurous traveller with hikes across suspension bridges, through caves and up to breathtaking viewpoints."},{type:"h2",text:"What Are the Tsingy?"},{type:"p",text:'The word tsingy means "where one cannot walk barefoot" in Malagasy — a fitting name for the needle-like limestone formations carved by millennia of rain. The park is divided into the Grand Tsingy (taller, more technical) and the Petit Tsingy (smaller, more accessible), each offering different trails and viewpoints.'},{type:"h2",text:"How to Get to Tsingy de Bemaraha"},{type:"p",text:"The gateway town is Bekopaka, reached by 4x4 from Morondava via Belo-sur-Tsiribihina, with two river ferry crossings along the way. The journey takes most of a day on rough dirt roads and is only possible in the dry season, roughly April to November."},{type:"tip",text:"Because of the ferry crossings and remote roads, a guided 4x4 tour is by far the easiest and safest way to reach Bekopaka. Going independently is slow and risky."},{type:"h2",text:"Grand Tsingy or Petit Tsingy?"},{type:"ul",items:["Grand Tsingy: taller pinnacles, harnesses, ladders and suspension bridges — for travellers comfortable with heights","Petit Tsingy: closer to Bekopaka, gentler trails with great viewpoints — suits most visitors","Most tours include both, so you can adapt on the day"]},{type:"h2",text:"How Difficult Is the Hike?"},{type:"p",text:"The Grand Tsingy circuit is physically demanding — you'll use fixed harnesses (via ferrata), climb ladders and squeeze through narrow passages. A reasonable level of fitness and no fear of heights helps. The Petit Tsingy is gentler and suits most visitors. Guides provide and fit all safety equipment."},{type:"h2",text:"What Wildlife Will You See?"},{type:"p",text:"Tsingy is home to endemic lemurs like the Decken's sifaka and red-fronted brown lemur, plus chameleons, birds and unique plants growing among the stone. The dramatic scenery is the star, but the wildlife makes it special."},{type:"h2",text:"Best Tours: Combine with Baobabs and Kirindy"},{type:"p",text:"Most travellers visit Tsingy as part of a 3 to 4 day western circuit that also includes the Avenue of the Baobabs and Kirindy Forest. This makes the long drive worthwhile and packs in Madagascar's western highlights — stone forest, wildlife and iconic baobabs — in a single trip."},{type:"tour",slug:"tsingy-de-bemaraha-3-day-express-circuit-madagascar",label:"Tsingy 3-Day Express Circuit",note:"For travellers short on time"},{type:"tour",slug:"tsingy-de-bemaraha-4-day-unesco-kirindy-baobabs",label:"Tsingy 4-Day Grand Circuit",note:"Tsingy, Kirindy and the baobabs"}],faq:[{q:"Is the Grand Tsingy difficult?",a:"It is physically demanding, with harnesses, ladders and narrow passages. Good fitness and no fear of heights help. The Petit Tsingy is easier."},{q:"When is Tsingy de Bemaraha open?",a:"It is usually accessible only in the dry season, roughly April to November. Roads are impassable in the rainy season."},{q:"How many days do you need for Tsingy?",a:"At least 3 days from Morondava. A 4-day circuit lets you add Kirindy Forest and the Avenue of the Baobabs."}]},{slug:"kirindy-forest-fossa-lemurs-guide",title:"How to See the Fossa & Lemurs in Kirindy Forest",excerpt:"Kirindy Forest is the best place in Madagascar to spot the elusive fossa, plus lemurs and nocturnal wildlife. Here's how to maximise your sightings.",cover:"/images/photos/forest-canopy-kiritour-madagascar-eb1d5.webp",date:"2026-06-05",updated:"2026-10-15",readTime:"6 min",category:"Wildlife",keywords:"kirindy forest, fossa madagascar, see fossa, lemurs morondava, night walk madagascar, kirindy wildlife",relatedTour:"kirindy-forest-fossa-lemurs-2-day-safari",relatedTourLabel:"Kirindy Forest 2-Day Safari",facts:[{label:"From Morondava",value:"≈ 60 km · 2 hours"},{label:"Famous for",value:"The fossa"},{label:"Best for fossa",value:"≈ October – December"},{label:"Tip",value:"Stay overnight for a night walk"}],content:[{type:"p",text:"Kirindy Forest is one of Madagascar's wildlife jewels — a dry deciduous forest near Morondava that offers the best chance in the country to see the fossa, the island's largest predator. Combined with several lemur species and rich nocturnal life, it's a must for wildlife lovers visiting the west."},{type:"h2",text:"The Fossa: Madagascar's Apex Predator"},{type:"p",text:"The fossa is a cat-like carnivore found only in Madagascar and the island's top predator. Kirindy is famous as the most reliable place to see one, especially around the research camp where they are sometimes spotted during the day. Patience and an experienced guide dramatically improve your chances."},{type:"h2",text:"When Is the Best Time to Visit?"},{type:"p",text:"The dry season (April to November) is best for both access and wildlife. Fossa sightings peak during their mating season, roughly October to December, when the animals are more active and visible. The roads are also passable in these months."},{type:"tip",text:"Fossa are unpredictable — no guide can guarantee a sighting. Spending a night in or near Kirindy, with both dusk and dawn walks, gives you the best odds."},{type:"h2",text:"Lemurs and Night Walks"},{type:"p",text:"Kirindy hosts several lemur species, including Verreaux's sifaka and the red-fronted brown lemur by day. But the night walk is the real highlight: with a headlamp and guide, you can spot the tiny mouse lemur (one of the world's smallest primates), chameleons, geckos and nocturnal birds."},{type:"h2",text:"What to Bring"},{type:"ul",items:["A headlamp with spare batteries for the night walk","Closed shoes and long trousers","Insect repellent","Binoculars and a camera with a good zoom"]},{type:"h2",text:"Day Trip or Overnight?"},{type:"p",text:"Kirindy is about 60 km northeast of Morondava, roughly 2 hours by 4x4, often combined with a sunset stop at the Avenue of the Baobabs on the way. A 1-day trip is possible, but staying overnight for a night walk and an early morning walk maximises wildlife sightings."},{type:"tour",slug:"kirindy-forest-1-day-tour-from-morondava",label:"Kirindy Forest Day Tour",note:"A full day from Morondava"},{type:"tour",slug:"kirindy-forest-fossa-lemurs-2-day-safari",label:"Kirindy 2–3 Day Safari",note:"Night walk + dawn walk for the best sightings"}],faq:[{q:"Can you see a fossa in Kirindy?",a:"Kirindy is the most reliable place in Madagascar to see one, but sightings are never guaranteed. An overnight stay improves your chances."},{q:"How far is Kirindy from Morondava?",a:"About 60 km, roughly 2 hours by 4x4."},{q:"Is a night walk worth it?",a:"Yes — it's the best way to see the mouse lemur, chameleons and other nocturnal wildlife."}]},{slug:"tsiribihina-river-descent-complete-guide",title:"Tsiribihina River Descent: Complete Guide 2026",excerpt:"A 2-3 day pirogue descent down the Tsiribihina is one of Madagascar's great adventures. Here's what to expect, when to go and how to prepare.",cover:"/images/photos/sacred-river-and-wild-camps-kiritour-madagascar-c7c52.webp",date:"2026-06-07",updated:"2026-10-15",readTime:"7 min",category:"Travel Guide",keywords:"tsiribihina river, pirogue descent madagascar, tsiribihina tour, miandrivazo, river camping madagascar, sakalava villages",relatedTour:"tsiribihina-river-3-day-pirogue-descent-madagascar",relatedTourLabel:"Tsiribihina River 3-Day Descent",facts:[{label:"Start",value:"Miandrivazo"},{label:"End",value:"Belo-sur-Tsiribihina"},{label:"Classic length",value:"3 days / 2 nights"},{label:"Best season",value:"April – November"}],content:[{type:"p",text:"Drifting down the Tsiribihina River in a traditional wooden pirogue is one of the most peaceful and authentic adventures in western Madagascar. Over two or three days you pass red cliffs, sacred Sakalava villages, waterfalls and untouched riverine forest, camping on wild sandbanks under a sky full of stars."},{type:"h2",text:"Where Does the Descent Start?"},{type:"p",text:"Most descents begin in Miandrivazo, a town reached by road from Antananarivo or Morondava. From there you board the pirogue and float gently downstream toward Belo-sur-Tsiribihina, where the river journey ends and a 4x4 continues to Kirindy or the baobabs."},{type:"h2",text:"How Many Days Do You Need?"},{type:"p",text:"The classic descent is three days and two nights, giving a relaxed pace with time for swimming, village visits and birdwatching. A faster two-day version is possible, while longer trips combine the river with Tsingy de Bemaraha and Kirindy Forest for a full western circuit."},{type:"h2",text:"What Is Daily Life Like on the River?"},{type:"p",text:"Days are slow and restful. Your crew paddles and steers while you relax, swim in calm pools and watch kingfishers and herons along the banks. Evenings are spent at camp on a sandbank, with simple Malagasy meals cooked over a fire and a sky bright with stars far from any city light."},{type:"tip",text:"Bring sun protection, a hat, a dry bag for electronics and a light layer for cool evenings. Your guide handles tents, cooking and all river logistics."},{type:"h2",text:"Respecting Sakalava Traditions"},{type:"p",text:"The Tsiribihina flows through Sakalava lands where many places and customs are sacred. Your guide will explain local fady (taboos) and how to behave in villages, so your visit is welcomed and respectful."},{type:"h2",text:"Best Time to Go"},{type:"p",text:"The dry season from April to November is ideal, with comfortable water levels and pleasant weather. Avoid the rainy season (December to March), when water levels and road access become unpredictable."},{type:"h2",text:"Combining the River with Tsingy and Baobabs"},{type:"p",text:"The Tsiribihina descent pairs naturally with the highlights of the Menabe region. Many travellers continue from Belo to Kirindy Forest for fossa and lemurs, then finish at the Avenue of the Baobabs at sunset — a perfect western Madagascar loop."},{type:"tour",slug:"tsiribihina-river-3-day-pirogue-descent-madagascar",label:"Tsiribihina River 3-Day Descent",note:"The classic pirogue journey"},{type:"tour",slug:"tsiribihina-tsingy-bemaraha-6-day-tour-madagascar",label:"River, Tsingy & Sunset — 6 Days",note:"River + Tsingy + Kirindy in one loop"}],faq:[{q:"Is the Tsiribihina descent comfortable?",a:"It's simple but relaxing: you sit in a wooden pirogue paddled by the crew and sleep in tents on sandbanks. The guide and crew handle all equipment and meals."},{q:"How long is the Tsiribihina river descent?",a:"The classic descent is 3 days and 2 nights from Miandrivazo to Belo-sur-Tsiribihina."},{q:"Can I combine the river with Tsingy?",a:"Yes — from Belo you can continue to Bekopaka for the Tsingy, then Kirindy and the baobabs. Our 6- and 8-day tours do exactly this."}]},{slug:"madagascar-itinerary-from-morondava",title:"Madagascar Itinerary: How to Plan Your Trip from Morondava",excerpt:"Morondava is the gateway to western Madagascar's icons. Here's how to plan a 3, 5 or 7+ day itinerary covering baobabs, Tsingy, Kirindy and the river.",cover:"/images/photos/complete-western-circuit-kiritour-madagascar-f085a.webp",date:"2026-06-09",updated:"2026-10-15",readTime:"8 min",category:"Itineraries",keywords:"madagascar itinerary, morondava travel, western madagascar trip, how many days madagascar, menabe circuit, madagascar trip planning",relatedTour:"western-madagascar-5-day-circuit-baobabs-kirindy",relatedTourLabel:"Western Madagascar 5-Day Circuit",facts:[{label:"1–2 days",value:"Baobabs, coast, Kirindy"},{label:"3–4 days",value:"+ Tsingy de Bemaraha"},{label:"5–8 days",value:"+ Tsiribihina River"},{label:"Season",value:"April – November"}],content:[{type:"p",text:"Morondava, on Madagascar's west coast, is the perfect base for exploring the wonders of the Menabe region — the Avenue of the Baobabs, Kirindy Forest, Tsingy de Bemaraha and the Tsiribihina River. How you combine them depends on how many days you have. Here's how to plan."},{type:"h2",text:"Short Trip: 1-2 Days"},{type:"p",text:"With limited time, focus on the icons closest to Morondava. A day tour to the Avenue of the Baobabs at sunset, combined with the nearby Kimony beach and Betania fishing village, gives a strong taste of the region. Add a day trip to Kirindy Forest for your first lemurs and a chance at spotting a fossa."},{type:"tour",slug:"avenue-baobabs-betania-kimony-day-tour-morondava",label:"Baobabs, Betania & Kimony Day Tour",note:"Perfect first day in Morondava"},{type:"h2",text:"Classic Trip: 3-4 Days"},{type:"p",text:"Three to four days is enough to reach Tsingy de Bemaraha, the UNESCO stone forest. A typical loop drives from Morondava to Bekopaka (with a baobabs stop), explores the Grand and Petit Tsingy, then returns via Kirindy Forest for wildlife and a final baobabs sunset."},{type:"tour",slug:"tsingy-de-bemaraha-4-day-unesco-kirindy-baobabs",label:"Tsingy 4-Day Grand Circuit",note:"Tsingy, Kirindy and the baobabs"},{type:"h2",text:"Complete Trip: 5-8 Days"},{type:"p",text:"With a week, you can add the Tsiribihina River descent to the circuit, combining a peaceful pirogue journey with the Tsingy, Kirindy and baobabs. This is the most complete western Madagascar experience, blending river, stone forest, wildlife and iconic landscapes."},{type:"tour",slug:"menabe-grand-tour-8-day-madagascar-adventure",label:"Epic Menabe Grand Tour — 8 Days",note:"The complete western loop"},{type:"tip",text:"Western Madagascar's roads are rough and remote, with river ferry crossings. A private 4x4 with a local guide saves time, handles logistics and is far safer than going independently."},{type:"h2",text:"When to Visit"},{type:"p",text:"Plan your trip in the dry season, April to November. The roads to Tsingy and Kirindy are only reliably passable in these months, and the weather is best for the baobabs at sunset. The rainy season can cut off access to the interior entirely."},{type:"h2",text:"How to Get to Morondava"},{type:"p",text:"Morondava has a small airport with flights from Antananarivo, or you can travel overland via Antsirabe and Miandrivazo. Once you arrive, a guided tour takes care of all transport and accommodation so you can focus on the experience rather than the logistics."}],faq:[{q:"How many days do you need in Morondava?",a:"At least 2 days for the baobabs and Kirindy, 3–4 days to add the Tsingy, and 5–8 days for the full loop with the Tsiribihina River."},{q:"How do you get to Morondava?",a:"By domestic flight from Antananarivo, or overland via Antsirabe and Miandrivazo (a long drive, usually split over two days)."}]},{slug:"what-to-pack-madagascar-safari",title:"What to Pack for a Madagascar Safari & Tour",excerpt:"Heading to Madagascar's baobabs, Tsingy and forests? This practical packing list covers clothing, gear and essentials for a comfortable western Madagascar trip.",cover:"/images/photos/indri-calls-and-night-walks-kiritour-madagascar-ff930.webp",date:"2026-06-11",updated:"2026-10-15",readTime:"6 min",category:"Travel Tips",keywords:"madagascar packing list, what to pack madagascar, madagascar safari gear, madagascar travel essentials, tsingy hiking gear",relatedTour:"tsingy-de-bemaraha-4-day-unesco-kirindy-baobabs",relatedTourLabel:"Tsingy de Bemaraha 4-Day Tour",facts:[{label:"Must-have",value:"Headlamp + hiking shoes"},{label:"Bag",value:"Soft duffel or backpack"},{label:"Money",value:"Cash in small notes"},{label:"Power",value:"Power bank (limited charging)"}],content:[{type:"p",text:"Packing well makes a big difference on a western Madagascar trip, where you'll move between hot baobab plains, sharp Tsingy limestone, humid forests and wild river camps. Here's a practical list of what to bring for comfort and safety."},{type:"h2",text:"Clothing"},{type:"ul",items:["Light, breathable quick-dry shirts in neutral colours","Long trousers to protect against sun, scratches and mosquitoes","Shorts for hot days in the west","A light fleece or jacket for cool mornings, evenings and the highlands","A hat and a buff or scarf against sun and dust"]},{type:"h2",text:"Footwear"},{type:"p",text:"Sturdy closed hiking shoes with good grip are essential, particularly for the Tsingy where you walk across sharp limestone. Bring sandals or flip-flops for camp and river crossings."},{type:"h2",text:"Sun & Insect Protection"},{type:"p",text:"The western sun is strong. Pack high-SPF sunscreen, lip balm, sunglasses and insect repellent. Long sleeves in the evening help against mosquitoes, especially near the river and forests."},{type:"tip",text:"A headlamp is essential for night walks in Kirindy and for camp on the Tsiribihina. Bring spare batteries — there's little or no electricity in remote areas."},{type:"h2",text:"Gear & Essentials"},{type:"ul",items:["Refillable water bottle","Dry bag to protect electronics on the river","Power bank — charging is limited in remote areas","Small first-aid kit with any personal medication","Camera with extra memory cards and batteries","Binoculars for lemurs and birds"]},{type:"h2",text:"Documents & Money"},{type:"p",text:"Carry your passport, travel insurance details and some cash in small denominations, as card payments are rare outside the cities. Keep digital and paper copies of important documents in case of loss."},{type:"h2",text:"Travel Light, Travel Smart"},{type:"p",text:"A medium backpack or soft duffel works best for 4x4 travel and pirogue trips, where space is limited. Your guide can advise on exactly what you'll need for your chosen tour, so you only carry what's useful."},{type:"ask",text:"Not sure what you need for your tour? Send us your itinerary and we'll tell you exactly what to pack."}],faq:[{q:"What shoes do I need for the Tsingy?",a:"Sturdy closed hiking shoes with a good grip — the limestone is sharp and the trails include ladders and narrow passages."},{q:"Do I need cash in Madagascar?",a:"Yes. Card payments are rare outside the cities, so carry cash in small denominations."}]}],t=Object.fromEntries(a.map(e=>[e.slug,e]));export{t as P,a as p};
